 did I sell I'm gonna keep 10  cents but I think for online food  delivery actually dates back to 1999  when a company called seamless web began  delivering to law firms in New York they  eventually merged with GrubHub but  concept caught on pretty slowly as  Maloney himself conceded as recently as  2012 I mean if you walk down the street  here and said what's GrubHub like no one  no one would know yeah so it was a lot  harder pitch back then paddy Barrett was  hired to help sell restaurants on the  idea in 2011 sure it's exciting first  job you call your parents yeah you say  I'm working for this thing called  GrubHub and they say what they thought I  said Groupon so it's my first selling  experience I guess you think you're  selling your parents don't worry yeah  it's really simple so yeah but then  something changed you pick the menu  items that you want to order from maybe  it was the rise of the smartphone Renae  and an online ordering mushroom spicy  lamb meatball sandwich by 2014 GrubHub  had gone public today it's worth four  and a half billion dollars but it's  battling for business with new  competitors with big investors behind  them including uber eats post mates and  biggest of them all door - which is  valued at more than twelve billion  dollars yeah these things yes all  different all different apps white maze  in Brooklyn New York uses almost all the  apps to sell their Venezuelan arepas and  rice bowls Kathy Valera bought the  restaurant in 2018 you seemed pretty  busy people walking in why even bother  being on these apps because when I get  the restaurant like a year and a half  ago it wasn't done busy the restaurant  was making barely a hundred dollars a  day then she signed up with GrubHub and  Thursday it was made $300 secondly it  was a far  Thursday like five hundreds on oh my god  you know today is $3,000 so that's why  I'm like in love with all the type of  apps that we have but all those orders  are not free what do you pay for grab  them 